This paper examines the importance of document accessibility, particularly in the context of digital documents. With the rise of COVID-19, there has been an abrupt shift towards using digital documents for acquiring knowledge and making informed decisions. Not all digital content, however, is optimized for accessibility, posing significant challenges to individuals with physical impairments. In response to this issue, we suggest setting up a benchmark dataset that showcases how accessible inaccessible documents can be made with the integration of specific artificial intelligence techniques, such as using artificial intelligence for table structuring and using speech-to-text software to generate text from images. Our primary goal is to highlight the need for document accessibility and to explore potential pathways to creating more inclusive digital environments. Furthermore, we aim to stimulate discussions and gather relevant research contributions that can ultimately bridge the gap in creating digitally inclusive and accessible content for all users, particularly for those who are print-challenged. A related GitHub repository is provided at https://github.com/ManuJadav123/DocAcc. Additionally, a video discussing document accessibility is available at https://youtu.be/cH1YrV0H3GQ. \thanks{Research supported by the ISIRD Scheme of NIT Delhi.} \vspace{0.2cm} \end{abstract}

